---
import DocLayout from '../layouts/DocLayout.astro';
import LegalIntro from '../components/LegalIntro.astro';
import EmailLink from '../components/EmailLink.astro';
import { site } from '../config/site';

const toc = [
  { id: 'provider', label: 'Your AI provider and model' },
  { id: 'data', label: 'What is sent to the AI' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'Limits of AI replies' },
  { id: 'people', label: 'People stay in charge' },
  { id: 'disclosure', label: 'Telling your customers' },
];
---

<DocLayout
  title="AI transparency"
  description="How AI works in Get Assist: workspaces choose their own AI provider and model, what content is sent for a reply, the limits of AI answers and handover to people."
  toc={toc}
>
  <div slot="intro">
    <LegalIntro title="AI transparency">
      <p class="text-slate">
        {site.brand} can use AI to suggest or write replies to customer messages. This page explains
        who provides the AI, what it sees, and where its limits are.
      </p>
    </LegalIntro>
  </div>

  <h2 id="provider">Your AI provider and model</h2>
  <p>
    {site.brand} does not force one AI model on every customer. Each workspace selects and
    configures its own AI provider, credentials and model from the options {site.brand} supports.{' '}
    {site.brand} provides the inbox, routing, settings and controls around it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each AI provider has its own terms, including whether it may use data to improve its models.
    Workspace owners should review the terms of the provider they choose.
  </p>

  <h2 id="data">What is sent to the AI</h2>
  <p>When a workspace turns on AI features, {site.brand} sends the selected provider:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>the customer’s message and relevant earlier messages in the conversation;</li>
    <li>the parts of the workspace’s knowledge base that relate to the question.</li>
  </ul>
  {site.commitments.aiIsolationVerified && (
    <p>
      Content is sent only to the provider that workspace configured, never to another workspace’s
      provider. If AI features are off, no message content is sent to an AI provider.
    </p>
  )}

  <h2 id="limits">Limits of AI replies</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>AI replies can be wrong, incomplete or out of date, even with a good knowledge base.</li>
    <li>The AI only knows what is in the conversation and the knowledge base.</li>
    <li>
      AI replies are not legal, medical, financial or safety advice, and should not be used for it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="people">People stay in charge</h2>
  <p>
    A person on the business’s team can take over a conversation at any time, with the earlier
    messages still in view. The business decides how AI is used in its workspace and is responsible
    for replies sent from it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="disclosure">Telling your customers</h2>
  <p>
    Where the law or a platform’s rules require it, businesses must tell their customers that
    replies may be generated by AI. This includes Meta’s rules for automated messaging on Messenger
    and Instagram.
  </p>
  <p>
    Questions: <EmailLink email={site.emails.general} />
  </p>
</DocLayout>
